bootable install diskettes
==========================

	make bootdisks

at the top of the tree builds three, here:

	d8ss.img	8 inch, single sided	77 cyl, 1 head,  15 x 512	1125 blocks
	d8ds.img	8 inch, double sided	77 cyl, 2 heads, 15 x 512	2250 blocks
	d5ds.img	5 1/4, double sided	40 cyl, 2 heads, 10 x 512	 760 blocks

They are build output, regenerated from the tree the way a .vol is, and
.gitignore drops them; the script that makes them is the only real file
in this directory.  Nothing here is the distributed standalone: see
REGEN in ../hdinstall for what patching that costs and for what is left
open there.

The five inch single sided medium is deliberately absent.  194 K is not
a running system, and a diskette that can only be a data carrier is not
worth a media row.

what one is
-----------

A whole small micronix, on one diskette, whose root is the diskette.
The loader is in the sectors in front of the filesystem and the
filesystem behind it, and one struct dlabel at byte 256 of block 0
describes both - so the diskette says what it is rather than the name it
is reached by.  A reader with the file in its hands and no device number
at all can mount it.  See docs/DISKLABEL.md for the label,
and src/micronix/stand/boot/README for the loader.

To boot one:

	cd ../../src/hwsim/d1
	./d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -B djdma -5 <the d5ds image>

The bare filename of an eight inch image goes on the eight inch port;
the five inch one needs -5.  Both are unskewed raw images - the DJ's
alternate sectoring bit is clear - which is what the loader and the host
tools both read.

what is on it, and why so little
--------------------------------

The install diskette is not a tool bag.  m16init mkfs's a hard disk,
mounts it on /b and copies the running system across with cptree, so
what has to fit is the smallest micronix that can do that, and the
medium it has to fit in is 760 blocks:

	/micronix	the kernel, 86 K, the largest thing here
	/bootmw.bin	the hard disk boot the install writes into the new
			drive's boot area - mkfs.h's DEFBOOT, read from the
			diskette that is running the install
	/m16init	the install script
	/.sh		the shell's path
	/bin		sh login ls cp mv rm cat mkdir mkfs fsck mount
			umount chmod cptree mknod setdev ln sync echo
	/dev		every node in ../../src/micronix/dev/devlist
	/etc		the config and init
	/b /f /tmp	the mount points m16init and finstall use, and a
			scratch directory

That is 724 of the 760 blocks on the five inch, leaving 17.  The eight
inch media carry the same files with the room they have over.  So the
knapsack is full: anything added to that list has to displace
something, which is the reason there is one m16init here and not all
four of the inits with a boot image each.

install, from the diskette
--------------------------

Boot it, wait for the welcome and then for the shell's "#", and type

	source m16init

which mkfs's /dev/m16a, fsck's it, mounts it on /b, copies the system
across with cptree, points the copy's kernel at the drive with setdev,
relinks /dev/root, and unmounts.  It prints "Disk initialization
complete." when it is done, and cptree is copying a filesystem through
a simulated Z80, so give it time.

Then boot the drive:

	./d1 -b roms/mon447.bin -c 0x0c hdcdma0:<volume>

which is the HD-DMA path with the monitor skipped - the configuration
switch 0x0c everything used to be written down as.  See ../hdinstall
for the volume to point it at and for the rest of that run.

"source m16init" rather than "m16init", because the console shell's path
is /bin and /usr/bin and m16init is in the root directory.  source is a
builtin and reads the file where you name it.

what is not here
----------------

	/.login		rootfiles/.login is "cd /usr/src; make" - the
			volume's build login, which on a diskette with no
			sources would only fail
	era		a Whitesmiths command this tree has never built; the
			install uses rm -f where the original used it
	a compiler, sources, man pages, and the rest of /bin - a system
	with a hard disk installs those from the media that carry them

the format byte, which is only on the five inch
------------------------------------------------

A hard sectored medium's geometry cannot be worked out from the drive;
the controller does not report it.  What dj.c does instead is read byte
0x5C of sector 0 - the level 0 boot that the firmware copies - and look
it up in its table of Morrow formats (sys/dj.c, specs[]).  d5ds carries
0xD0: 40 cylinders, two heads, ten sectors, 512 bytes.  A diskette
without it does not fail to boot - the driver quietly keeps its default
row for a five inch drive, 35 cylinders one head, and reads the first
330 filesystem blocks correctly and nothing after them, which is exactly
where the kernel's overlay modules are.  The symptom is a mounted
filesystem, a healthy-looking loader, "ovl: 0 not placed" and then a
panic.

The eight inch media are soft sectored and the driver never looks, so
they carry zero.  src/micronix/stand/boot/GNUmakefile's FLOPPIES table
has the byte per medium, and mkbootimg writes it and the Makefile reads
it back; the whole story is in mkbootimg.c's DJCONFIG.

